mpares `val_dep.bool()` (CPU) with `calc_preds(...) > 0.5` (on device), causing a RuntimeError on GPU. Fix: `t_dep = tensor(df.Survived, device=device)`.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "Target tensor `t_dep` not on `device` causes device mismatch during evaluation"}, {"body": "Three `init_coeffs` definitions (Cells 82, 135, 144) do NOT specify `device=device`, creating weights on CPU: Cell 82 uses `torch.rand(n_coeff)`, Cell 135 uses `torch.rand(n_coeff, 1)`, Cell 144 uses `torch.rand(n_coeff, n_hidden)`, `torch.rand(n_hidden, 1)`, `torch.rand(1)`. Only Cell 157 correctly uses `device=device`. When these CPU weights are used with `t_indep` on GPU (Cell 37) in `calc_loss`, the matrix multiplication fails with a device mismatch RuntimeError. Fix: add `device=device` to all `init_coeffs` definitions.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "Multiple `init_coeffs` definitions missing `device=device` causing GPU failures"}, {"body": "In Cell 118, test data is processed with `get_dummies` and then `tst_df[indep_cols]` is indexed using the hardcoded column list: `['Survived', 'Age', 'SibSp', 'Parch', 'LogFare', 'Sex_male', 'Sex_female', 'Pclass_1', 'Pclass_2', 'Pclass_3', 'Embarked_C', 'Embarked_Q', 'Embarked_S']`. The `get_dummies` function only creates columns for categories present in the data. If the test set lacks certain categories present in training (e.g., no 'Q' port passengers), `Embarked_Q` column won't exist in `tst_df`, and `tst_df[indep_cols]` will raise a KeyError. Fix: ensure all expected columns exist or use sparse encoding.", "confidence": 0.9, "title": "Test data `get_dummies` may produce fewer columns than training data causing KeyError"}, {"body": "Training data fills NaN values in the Fare column with `modes['Fare']` (Cell 15: `df.fillna(modes, inplace=True)`), but test data fills NaN values in Fare with 0 (Cell 116: `tst_df['Fare'] = tst_df.Fare.fillna(0)`). This preprocessing inconsistency means training and test data handle missing Fare differently. If `modes['Fare'] != 0`, the model receives differently-processed inputs for train vs test, leading to incorrect predictions. Fix: apply the same `fillna(modes, inplace=True)` to test Fare, or remove Cell 116 entirely.", "confidence": 0.95, "title": "Preprocessing inconsistency: Fare NaN filled with mode in training but with 0 in test"}]}


