{"findings": [{"title": "Data leakage from feature normalization computed on entire dataset before train/val split", "body": "In Cell 36, `t_indep = tensor(df[indep_cols].values, dtype=torch.float)` loads ALL features from the entire dataframe. In Cell 45, `vals,indices = t_indep.max(dim(0))` computes the maximum of ALL rows and `t_indep = t_indep / vals` normalizes ALL data. The train/val split via `RandomSplitter(seed=42)(df)` in Cell 72 happens AFTER normalization. The split is then applied to the already-normalized data in Cell 77 (`trn_indep = t_indep[trn_split]`). This means validation row statistics (the max values) leak into training data preprocessing. Fix: Split into train/val first, compute `vals` from training data only, then apply normalization to both sets.", "confidence": 1.0}, {"title": "Data leakage from mode imputation computed on entire dataset before train/val split", "body": "In Cell 12, `modes = df.mode().iloc[0]` computes the mode from the ENTIRE dataframe including rows that will later become validation data. In Cell 14, `df.fillna(modes, inplace=True)` imputes missing values using these global statistics. The train/val split via `RandomSplitter(seed=42)(df)` in Cell 72 happens AFTER imputation, meaning validation data was used to compute imputation statistics that affect training data. Fix: Split into train/val first, compute modes from training data only, then apply imputation to both sets.", "confidence": 0.9}, {"title": "Non-deterministic accuracy function prevents reproducibility", "body": "The `acc` function in Cell 96 uses `resample = torch.rand_like(preds.float()) < 0.03` to randomly flip ~3% of predictions. Since no random seed is set before this call, `torch.rand_like` generates different random values on each invocation, making the accuracy metric non-deterministic. Running the same evaluation twice on the same model produces different results. Fix: Remove the random resampling, or if intentional, set a fixed seed before the random operation.", "confidence": 0.8}, {"title": "Hardcoded column names cause KeyError when test set lacks categories from training", "body": "In Cell 32, `added_cols` is hardcoded as `['Sex_male', 'Sex_female', 'Pclass_1', 'Pclass_2', 'Pclass_3', 'Embarked_C', 'Embarked_Q', 'Embarked_S']`. In Cell 117, `tst_df = pd.get_dummies(tst_df, columns=['Sex','Pclass','Embarked'])` creates dummy columns based only on categories present in the test set. If the test set lacks any category (e.g., no passengers with Embarked='Q'), that column won't be created, and `tst_df[indep_cols]` will raise a `KeyError` since `indep_cols` contains all hardcoded column names. Fix: Dynamically determine which columns were created by get_dummies on training data, then ensure test data has matching columns by filling missing ones with zeros.", "confidence": 0.85}]}


